      "featured": false,
      "body": "**Roofing in Yakima, WA** comes with challenges that homeowners from wetter parts of Washington rarely expect. Yakima Valley summers bring high-desert heat, heavy UV, and steady winds that test a roof from multiple directions at once. After 30 years in residential construction, Terry Preston of High Point Renovation has learned that roofing in Yakima requires a different conversation than roofing in a cooler, cloudier market. This guide covers the five things every Yakima homeowner should understand: how the local climate shortens roof life, how to tell repair from replacement, what to look for in a contractor, what shingle options fit the valley, and what a real free inspection covers.\n\n## What Yakima's Climate Does to Your Roof\n\nThe Yakima Valley sits in the rain shadow east of the Cascades. That means the same dry air and reliable sun that make it one of Washington's best growing regions also put extra strain on a roof year-round.\n\nSummer temperatures in Yakima regularly push past 100 degrees on south-facing slopes. That sustained heat dries out the asphalt binder inside each shingle and loosens the granules that protect it from UV. At night, temperatures drop sharply - sometimes by 40 degrees before morning. That constant expansion and contraction cycles the shingles and their sealant bonds year after year without a break.\n\nWind adds to the problem. Yakima's valley geography channels Columbia Basin winds in ways that can surprise homeowners who moved from the wetter west side of the state. A steady afternoon blow does not need to reach storm speed to lift a shingle edge that has already lost its sealant bond from years of heat cycling.\n\nThe practical result is that a roof in Yakima ages faster than the manufacturer's warranty chart assumes. Those charts are built for a national average climate, not a high-desert one, and a roof that would last 25 years in a mild coastal climate may need attention several years earlier here.\n\n## Signs You Need Repair vs. Replacement\n\nThe most common question Yakima homeowners ask is whether their roof needs a repair or a full replacement. The answer depends on where the damage is and how far it has spread.\n\nA repair is usually right when the problem is isolated: a lifted shingle on one section, a failed flashing around a chimney or vent, or a small area of granule loss that has not yet reached the mat underneath. A good repair on a roof that still has useful life left will hold for years in Yakima's climate.\n\nReplacement becomes the right call when the wear has spread across multiple slopes, when large areas of the shingles have lost their granule coverage, or when the deck underneath has taken on moisture. A spongy or sagging roof line is a sign the damage has already moved past the surface. At that point, patching does not fix the structure underneath, and the repair cycle becomes more expensive with each passing season.\n\nAge matters too. A roof pushing past 15 to 20 years in Yakima's climate has typically spent most of its useful life, even if it has not leaked yet. Planning a [roof replacement](/services/roof-replacement) before a failure happens is nearly always less disruptive and less expensive than responding to one after the fact.\n\n## Choosing a Roofing Contractor in Yakima\n\nYakima is roughly 90 miles from the Tri-Cities, and not every roofing crew that serves the valley is equipped to handle that distance reliably. A few things worth checking before you hire anyone:\n\nFirst, confirm they are a licensed and insured Washington contractor. You can look up a contractor's registration with the state Department of Labor and Industries. A legitimate roofer does not hesitate to provide their registration number so you can verify it yourself.\n\nSecond, ask for a written estimate before any work starts. A roofer who names a number out loud and adjusts it after the tear-off has begun is not someone to trust with your home. At High Point, the written estimate you receive is the price you pay - no change-order games, no line items that appear mid-project.\n\nThird, ask what goes under the shingles. The hidden materials - underlayment, flashings, ice-and-water shield in the valleys - are where a cheaper bid typically cuts. Once the shingles are back down, you cannot inspect them.\n\nTerry Preston has 30 years in residential and commercial construction and personally manages and oversees every project. You are not handed off to someone who was not part of the original conversation.\n\n## Asphalt Shingle Options for the Yakima Valley\n\nFor Yakima's climate, a heavier architectural shingle holds up better than a standard three-tab under the same heat and UV exposure. The added thickness and stronger granule bond provide more resistance to the kind of accelerated wear Yakima summers produce year after year.\n\nColor matters more in a high-desert climate than in a cooler one. Lighter shingles reflect more solar radiation and run cooler on the surface, which extends the life of the asphalt binder and can help lower summer cooling costs. For most Yakima homes, a medium or lighter tone is a better long-term choice than a dark one.\n\nAs an IKO ROOFPRO SELECT contractor, High Point installs IKO shingles on replacements across our service area, including Yakima. If a payment plan helps the project fit your budget, we are an Improvifi Certified Contractor - financing is subject to credit approval, and we provide the written estimate first so you know the exact number before you apply for anything.\n\n## What a Free Inspection Covers in Yakima\n\nA free roof inspection from Terry is a hands-on review of the points that actually tell you what condition your roof is in - not a quick look from the driveway.\n\nOn a Yakima property, we check the shingles across all slopes for granule loss and curling, with particular attention to the south and west faces that take the most direct sun. We look carefully at the valleys and flashings, which are the most common points of failure and the hardest spots to see from the ground. We check the gutters for granule buildup, which is an early sign of surface wear. And we look for signs of inadequate attic ventilation, since a roof can deteriorate faster from inside when trapped heat has nowhere to go.\n\nYou get a written report with photos of what we found. A [roof inspection](/services/roof-inspections) either confirms your roof is in good shape or gives you a clear picture of what it needs, with no pressure and no obligation. If the roof needs a repair, that is what we quote. If a replacement is the honest answer, we show you the evidence and give you a firm written price.\n\nLearn more about our [Yakima roofing service area](/service-areas/yakima), or [contact us](/contact) to book a free inspection. Terry gets back to you the same day.",
